                            Preamble

  The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure
cooperation with the community in the case of network server software.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
the GNU Affero General Public License is intended to guarantee your
freedom to share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it
remains free software for all its users.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
them if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you
want it, that you can change the software or use pieces of it in new
free programs, and that you know you can do these things.

  Developers that use the GNU Affero General Public License protect your
rights with two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2)
offer you this License which gives you legal permission to copy,
distribute and/or modify the software.

  A secondary benefit of defending all users' freedom is that
improvements made in alternate versions of the program, if they receive
widespread use, become available for the other developers to
incorporate.  Many developers of free software are heartened and
encouraged by the resulting cooperation.  However, in the case of
software used on network servers, this result may fail to come about.
The GNU General Public License permits making a modified version and
letting the public access it on a server without ever releasing its
source code to the public.

  The GNU Affero General Public License addresses this problem by
offering you the additional permission to link the program (or works
based on it) with independently created modules that communicate with
it by design to perform a service, provided that those modules are not
wired to provide services under the terms of the Affero GPL (i.e., the
connection is not between your code and theirs, but rather between
theirs and yours), and you wish to extend the rights normally granted
under the Affero GPL to modules (or works based on them) that do
perform this communication.  You may copy and distribute such a system
following the terms of the GNU Affero General Public License for the
program plus the additional permission above, provided that you modify
that text to reflect the actual circumstances of your situation.

  When you use the GNU Affero General Public License to cover a work, you
have certain responsibilities to respect the freedom of others:

    * You must make the source code of the work available to the users of
      the work under the Affero GPL (either that version or, at your
      option, any later version published by the Free Software
      Foundation).

    * You must prominently display the fact that the work is regulated
      by the Affero GPL, and inform users of that fact.

    * You must convey to each user of the work a copy of the Affero GPL
      along with the work.

    * You must include a mechanism to request the source code of the work
      from a network server that provides the work to network users.

    * For any version of the program that supports user interaction
      through a computer network, you must also ensure that user
      interaction through that network will provide a way to request the
      source code of the program.

    * If you modify the program, those modifications must also be subject
      to the Affero GPL, and you must provide source code to all users of
      the modified program.

  Please note that user-facing network services that use a modified
version of the program to communicate with remote computers must follow
the Affero GPL's clause about user interaction through computer
networks.

