the source code needed to generate, install, and (for an executable
work) run the object code and to modify the work, including scripts to
control those activities.  However, it does not include the work's
System Libraries, or general-purpose tools or general application
programs that are typically distributed (with source or binary) with
the major components (operating system, compiler, library) on which
the executable work runs, unless that component itself accompanies
the executable work.  If this is an executable work, the Corresponding
Source includes the Corresponding Source for any works that it is
derived from, as well as the source code for compiling and linking
the included compilation units, and also the source code for all
changes made to the work, and all associated General Public License
complying installation scripts.  However, as a special exception, the
source code distributed need not include anything that is normally
distributed with the major components (operating system, compiler,
library) on which the executable work runs, unless that component
itself accompanies the executable work.  If distribution of object
code is made by offering access from a designated place, then offering
equivalent access to copy the source code from the same place counts
as distribution of the source code, even though third parties are not
compelled to copy along with the object code.  If you distribute
copies of an executable work, or works based on the work, you must
give the recipients of the work all the source code for the work in
object code or executable form under the terms of the Affero GPL,
